article {


hgroup {
    h1 { AI 2026 }

    Where we are, and where we aren't
}
hr {}

section#intro {

In October 2026, when I began writing this, OpenAI had just released a[href=https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics/]{a massive GitHub repo of .pdf and .lean files}.

There were more than 700 PDFs, and a substantial subset of them were even associated with Lean certificates.

This led to quite a bit of discussion. Is it the end of human mathematics? Is it time to move to a $scare{math 2.0}? Is AGI here? Or ASI? The eschaton?

With all the commentary flying around, much of it quite bombastic, I figured I should go ahead and make a record of where my head was at at the time. In the process of writing, the scope of the post expanded quite a bit beyond just mathematics. It's become I guess an attempt at making legible a considerable portion of my thinking on AI more broadly. Those who follow me on BlueSky and are pious enough to read all my posts every day will probably already have seen many of these takes, albeit with a little less polish and context. In keeping with the aphoristic spirit of a poster, I don't intend this as a persuasive piece, more of a snapshot of my thoughts. I have structured it as a dialogue with a fictional interrogator, whose primary purpose is to bring up various points I've seen on social media that I want to address here, and whose secondary purpose is to say whatever is most convenient for me so I can take the $scare{conversation} wherever I want it to go.

While I was assembling these thoughts I ended up reading William Thurston's famous essay a[href=https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9404236]{On Proof and Progress in Mathematics}, and as it turns out some parts of what I wanted to say about mathematics were quite similar to what Thurston had already capably put to words before I was even born. While I hadn't read it myself before I wrote up the bulk of this, my thinking has doubtlessly been greatly influenced by it, albeit indirectly. Anyway, I think it's quite good, and its author has significantly more $scare{math cred} than I probably ever will. If you only have time to read one essay today, I encourage you to go read that one.

Finally, I must note that when I was nearing the end of the editing of this piece, Levent Alpöge announced a claim of a Claude-assisted and Lean-formalized proof of the notorious Riemann Hypothesis. Regardless of how reviews of it shake out, I can already say I appreciate Alpöge's general approach to communicating the result much more than I appreciate OpenAI's, for reasons I think will be made clear enough by what I've already written.

}

