h3{I'd love to hear you ramble about the axiom of choice.}

Very well, I'll oblige. Many (all? I haven't checked) of the Lean-formalized results appear to take on the axiom of choice. I'd be more impressed if they didn't.

under a pluralism-of-foundations / pluralism-of-axiom-systems view, mathematical "truths" are situational, provisional truths; a claim about groups is true *insofar as* the things we are talking about really are *like groups*, i.e. *insofar as* they obey the group axioms

and things that obey the axiom of choice are... few and far between. it's just a bit of an alien requirement to impose on a domain! and that makes it less interesting to me than, say, group axioms, bc there's LOTS of interesting stuff that acts like groups. there's not that much stuff where i actually have a magic impossible-to-access choice function

see i think "universes" of sets are a deranged ontology to really believe in lmfao

now tbc practicality of zfc is not necessarily a strike against it, and none of what im saying is "nobody should work with choice". i disdain the idea that math must earn its keep in any way whatsoever :)

what it is a strike against is the foundational ambitions that ppl seem utterly unwilling to let go of despite their philosophical bankruptcy

part of the justification of choice has always been that it *lets us prove more things*, regardless of to what sort of circumstances those proofs pertain; in that sense it's always been a "slop axiom", something included for the sake of just *churning out results*

but it does matter which results really *need* choice and which don't. it matters which results hold under what conditions.

zfc's results hold in situations where zfc's axioms hold. but what are these situations? it's an alien theory.

and insofar as it produces results that are useful in real domains, it's a lot like if a strategist drew up war plans which all just sort of took it for granted that we had access to the legendary sword Excalibur, but then the role Excalibur plays in some of those plans is not so vital, or some more mundane implement can fill its role to a sufficient extent. So the assumption that we have a magic sword doesn't make the strategist's work entirely useless, but there are clear downsides to a state of affairs where it becomes the standard to always casually make that assumption.





you can encode group theoretic axioms in zfc, that doesn't mean that group theorists are all "using zfc", any more than it means they're all using any other foundation that could encode their work.




distinguishing my insofar-as concept from em{models}:

a em{model} of some axiom system is a mathematical structure that exactly satisfies the axioms.

what i am concerned with is a em{broader, less formal} class: em{things}, not em{necessarily} mathematical structures, which em{to some extent} satisfy the axioms; and that $scare{to some extent} is itself a fuzzy notion. it could mean $scare{in some contexts} or $scare{at some scales}, &c.

maybe we could call these $scare{quasimodels} or something. i'm talking philosophy of math, not metamathematics.

like, a rubiks cube is a quasimodel of the rubiks cube group for instance. "quasi" because you can manipulate pieces in illegal ways, or even fully take it apart, &c, but it remains provisionally useful to think of it in terms of the rubiks cube group and one is not committing some crime against rigor in doing so

my "insofar-as" captures this:

the formalism lets us know things about its quasimodel insofar as that quasimodel adheres to the formalism







that most ordinary math can be encoded set-theoretically is of little more interest than the fact that you can implement just about any program in python



}


section {

$header[endnote|numberless]{
    Endnote
}

h3{So what's your stake in all this anyway? Are you even a mathematician?}

As I said earlier on, I'm not an academic mathematician. As far as credentials go, I only have a bachelor's degree in it, and I don't really do any significant mathematics at my programming job. I do however spend a considerable amount of my free time studying mathematics and engaging in mathematical investigations. I also know a thing or two about machine learning, and have kept an eye on developments in it for some time. Much of what's at stake for me here is, I guess, is that the social media environment I exist in has been quite saturated with talk of AI for quite some time, the economy in which I reside has decided to invest quite a lot of its resources into AI. And while I'm not a mathematician, my own profession as a programmer has em{also}, from what I've been told, been made entirely obsolete by AI (though my employer hasn't noticed yet, so the lights stay on for now).

h3{This narrative device of the $scare{interrogator} lends an impression that you've pre-empted a great deal of criticism, but you haven't really. I'm an impotent strawman. Not my will, but thine, be done.}

That's a comment, not a question, and furthermore I find that sort of critically-self-aware break of the narrative device indulgent and distasteful.

h3{TL;DR?}

Read the Zhuangzi.

}


}





