TODO maybe cut or reduce this bit, it is slightly breaking the vow

I've made a vow never to make a long-form post about vibecoding, but this post is about AI more broadly so I feel it's fine to say a few words on it: How much benefit is there, really, in getting swept up in feelings of grandiose potential all while you have to keep paying 200 dollars a month to companies run by reactionary billionaires in order to feel able to continue work on your software projects? As far as I can see there hasn't been any corresponding explosion in the quantity of good software out there. It often seems as if those experiencing the feelings of power and potential have forgotten that people could already do impressive things with code em{before} AI hit the scene. Software engineers have always been quick to become die-hard evangelists for new tools and much of the talk about LLMs has the same character as can be seen in other SWE fads of the past and present.

On the other hand you have the $scare{AI is never good for anything} types. I think that's also a ridiculous position. There are plenty of things AI is useful for. Even the free tier models are useful.

A lot of people tie their identity up in either their embrace or rejection of AI, and I think that's a tendency one ought to make a conscious effort to overcome in either case.

