h3{This post is great, but I don't think it has enough politics yet.} TODO rewrite to be a little more cool-headed while getting the point across

I agree wholeheartedly. Let me remedy that. One litmus test for a widely-used technology being dubbed $scare{AGI} should be: is its integration into societal systems making those systems more generally intelligent? Are the people that embrace AI most fully behaving more $scare{intelligently} than the rest? A world where anyone can have a machine print out a Lean proof they don't understand to answer a question they don't understand, while we still wage wars and burn fossil fuels, while men who assert patriarchy to be the will of the divine seize control of political institutions, where discredited racist pseudoscience makes a comeback among those that style themselves $scare{rationalists}, where ICE rounds people up to put in camps and we send bombs halfway around the world to be dropped on schools and hospitals. All this shit isn't $scare{orthogonal} to intelligence: Misogyny is stupid. Fascism is stupid. Racism is fucking stupid. This moment in history is so goddamn fucking stupid, and sadly it doesn't seem like AI is helping correct for that to any significant degree. This is an age of rampant epistemic vice, and some of those who revel most wildly in such vice are enthusiastic adopters of AI. It is making them more effective at rapidly accomplishing tasks in certain domains, but it is not markedly improving their general ability to understand the world they live in, take established science seriously, place political currents in continuity with their histories, think at a broader scale than personal greed, escape perverse incentives, &c &c. The models aren't being trained to help people do any of that, because that's not what the labs understand to be part of intelligence. The major US labs are taken with the ideology that all of that amounts to em{values}, entirely unrelated to intelligence. But their devotion to neutrality goes a[href=https://github.com/anthropics/political-neutrality-eval/blob/main/eval_set.csv]{far beyond up-in-the-air matters of ethical philosophy, and extends to training their models not to take sides on matters of settled science when doing so would upset the American far right}.

